Dysmenorrhoea
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Cyclic crampy lower abdominal pain, Radiation to lower back/thighs, Normal pelvic examination

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-011
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, incapacitating cyclic lower abdominal pain and uncontrollable vomiting causing dehydration risk.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and mother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated sudden onset of excruciating, intractable menstrual cramps accompanied by continuous vomiting, preventing oral hydration or analgesia. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to administer parenteral antiemetics and analgesics to prevent profound dehydration and to rule out an acute surgical abdomen before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The period cramps are so bad I cannot stand up, and I have vomited every time I try to take a painkiller.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced aggressively 6 hours ago, synchronously with the onset of her menstrual flow. The patient reports severe, recurrent, crampy lower abdominal pain that radiates intensely to the lower back and down the anterior thighs. The pain is accompanied by severe nausea, continuous vomiting, and frequent bowel movements (diarrhea) coincident with the menses. She reports an associated headache and generalized fatigue. This predictable cyclic pattern occurs monthly, but this episode is remarkably severe, rendering oral NSAIDs impossible to keep down.
Functional Status: Bedbound, curled in a fetal position, functionally incapacitated by pain and nausea.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk for acute dehydration secondary to intractable vomiting.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/68 mmHg | HR: 104 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 55 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, diaphoretic, and in visible, acute distress, clutching her lower abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals diffuse, severe lower abdominal tenderness without guarding, rigidity, or rebound. Bowel sounds are hyperactive. Pelvic examination (external inspection) shows normal menstrual flow; bimanual exam deferred due to acute distress and classic primary presentation, but suprapubic palpation confirms absence of focal adnexal masses.
Environmental Observations: Emesis basin by the bed; mother providing physical support and cool cloths.
Medication Reconciliation: Attempted ibuprofen 400mg x2 doses, both vomited within 10 minutes.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Primary Dysmenorrhoea - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Appendicitis - Ruled out due to the bilateral, cyclic nature of the pain, absence of right lower quadrant point tenderness (McBurney's), and lack of fever.

Ovarian Torsion - Unlikely given the diffuse, crampy pain radiating to the thighs and back, rather than a sharp, sudden unilateral pain.

Endometriosis - Unlikely to be the primary cause in this acute presentation without a history of deep dyspareunia or chronic, non-cyclic pelvic pain, though remains a long-term differential.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, incapacitating cyclic lower abdominal cramping radiating to the thighs, complicated by intractable vomiting that prevents the administration of standard oral analgesia. The clinical objective evidence of diffuse lower abdominal tenderness without peritonism, combined with tachycardia secondary to pain and early dehydration, confirms an acute flare of primary dysmenorrhoea. The inability to tolerate oral intake necessitated urgent after-hours evaluation for the administration of intramuscular antiemetics and NSAIDs. This intervention could not be delayed until the next in-hours period without risking progressive dehydration requiring hospital admission for intravenous fluids.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ketorolac 30mg for pain and intramuscular metoclopramide 10mg for nausea. Prescribed oral mefenamic acid 500mg three times daily (to commence once vomiting has ceased for 2 hours) and ondansetron 4mg orally as needed.
Positive Behavioral Change: To establish rapid pain control in future cycles, begin taking NSAIDs 24 hours before the expected onset of menses; experiencing the prevention of severe pain will reinforce proactive, scheduled dosing. To soothe acute muscle spasms, continuously apply a hot water bottle to the lower abdomen or lower back; feeling the physical relaxation of the muscles will encourage reliance on topical heat as a primary adjunct therapy.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heating pad recommended.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in one month to discuss commencing continuous oral contraceptives for long-term suppression.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if the vomiting restarts and you cannot keep fluids down for more than 6 hours, or if the pain suddenly becomes sharp, localized to one side, and severe despite the injection.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis assumes a primary etiology; persistent severe symptoms may require ultrasound to rule out secondary causes.
Clinical Course: The injection should stop the vomiting within 30 minutes and significantly blunt the pain within an hour. You must start sipping water slowly once the nausea settles.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you become unable to hold down fluids again or if you develop a fever.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your body is producing too many prostaglandins, which cause the uterus to spasm violently and trigger vomiting and diarrhea. The injection directly blocks these chemicals, stopping the cramps and the nausea simultaneously so you can rehydrate.
Actionable Steps: Sip water slowly now. Once your stomach is settled, start taking the prescribed mefenamic acid with food, and apply heat to your stomach continuously.

SMS INFORMATION

Presumptive diagnosis: Severe Dysmenorrhoea. IM pain and nausea relief given. Start oral meds when stomach settles.

SMS EDUCATION

Take mefenamic acid with food. Use a heating pad. Go to ED if vomiting restarts and lasts >6 hours or pain becomes one-sided.
